      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 4250 mg three times daily comes to 12,750 mg/day, which is about 5 times the usual maximum daily dose of metformin (2,550 mg/day, or 2,000 mg/day for the extended-release form). A single 4250 mg dose is also far above the typical maximum of 1,000 mg per dose. At that level the risk of lactic acidosis is serious, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nThis may be a typo. Some doses you might have meant:\n- **850 mg** PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day, the maximum)\n- **425 mg** PO three times daily with meals, if you want a lower dose\n- **500 mg** or **1000 mg** PO twice daily with meals, which are common regimens\n\nTell me the dose you want and I'll enter it right away. If 4250 mg is really what you intend, for example as part of a protocol I'm not aware of, please confirm. I'd suggest checking with pharmacy first."
      }
     ],
